    <h2>Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>
  </div>

  <p>The 2020s data through March 2026 reveals a market in transition. After the pandemic crash and recovery (2020–2022), prices have entered a moderating trend — declining from over $110/bbl in mid-2022 to the $58–$73 range through most of 2025. The sharp spike to $87.84 in March 2026 introduces renewed uncertainty about near-term direction.</p>

  <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>

  <div class="decade-card" style="border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);">
    <p><strong style="color:var(--text);">1. Crude oil is a structurally volatile asset.</strong> The overall standard deviation of $30.59 represents 77% of the mean price — an extraordinary coefficient of variation. No decade has been immune to shocks of 30%+ magnitude.</p>
    <p><strong style="color:var(--text);">2. Price regimes persist, then break violently.</strong> The 1990s stability ($18.19 ± $3.82) collapsed into the 2000s supercycle ($49.17 ± $25.54). The 2011–2014 plateau above $100 broke to $30 in 18 months. Mean-reversion assumptions require time horizons of 5–10 years.</p>
    <p><strong style="color:var(--text);">3. Geopolitical events remain the dominant driver of tail risk.</strong> Of the 10 largest monthly price moves, nearly all are directly attributable to geopolitical events (embargoes, wars, revolutions) or macroeconomic crises. Supply-demand fundamentals set the trend; geopolitics determines the shocks.</p>
    <p><strong style="color:var(--text);">4. The 7.93% CAGR masks extreme path dependency.</strong> An investor exposed to this CAGR would have endured multiple drawdowns exceeding 60%, including 1985–1986 (−64%), 2008 (−69%), 2014–2016 (−72%), and 2020 (−66%). The long-term trend upward is real but the path is punishing.</p>
    <p><strong style="color:var(--text);">5. Recent moderation may not signal a new low-price era.</strong> The 2025 decline to $57.95 remains above the long-term median of $29.11 and is consistent with mid-cycle correction rather than structural repricing. The March 2026 spike demonstrates how quickly supply disruptions or demand shifts can reverse a declining trend.</p>
